## Proof

**Setup via fractional calculus.** Define the Riemann–Liouville half-integral
$$(I^{1/2}h)(x)=\frac{1}{\Gamma(1/2)}\int_0^x\frac{h(t)}{\sqrt{x-t}}\,dt=\frac{1}{\sqrt\pi}\int_0^x\frac{h(t)}{\sqrt{x-t}}\,dt.$$
The hypothesis on $f\in\mathcal F$ is exactly
$$\bigl|(I^{1/2}f)(x)\bigr|\le \frac{1}{\sqrt\pi},\qquad x\in(0,1].$$
Set $g:=I^{1/2}f$. Then $g$ is continuous, $g(0)=0$, and $|g(x)|\le 1/\sqrt\pi$.

**Relating the target to $g$.** By the semigroup property $I^{1/2}I^{1/2}=I^1$,
$$\int_0^1 f(x)\,dx=(I^1 f)(1)=(I^{1/2}g)(1)=\frac{1}{\sqrt\pi}\int_0^1\frac{g(t)}{\sqrt{1-t}}\,dt. \tag{1}$$

**Upper bound.** Since $|g(t)|\le 1/\sqrt\pi$,
$$\left|\int_0^1 f\right|\le\frac{1}{\sqrt\pi}\int_0^1\frac{|g(t)|}{\sqrt{1-t}}\,dt\le\frac{1}{\pi}\int_0^1\frac{dt}{\sqrt{1-t}}=\frac{1}{\pi}\cdot 2=\frac{2}{\pi}. \tag{2}$$

**Achievability (the bound is sharp).** We exhibit $f\in\mathcal F$ with $\int_0^1 f$ arbitrarily close to $2/\pi$.

For $\delta>0$, choose a $C^\infty$ function $g_\delta:[0,1]\to[0,1/\sqrt\pi]$ with $g_\delta(0)=0$, $g_\delta(t)=1/\sqrt\pi$ for $t\ge\delta$, and $0\le g_\delta\le 1/\sqrt\pi$ throughout. (For instance, ramp smoothly from $0$ to $1/\sqrt\pi$ on $[0,\delta]$.)

Since $g_\delta$ is $C^1$ with $g_\delta(0)=0$, the half-derivative
$$f_\delta(x):=(D^{1/2}g_\delta)(x)=\frac{d}{dx}(I^{1/2}g_\delta)(x)$$
is continuous on $[0,1]$ (standard regularity: $I^{1/2}$ maps $C^1_0$ functions to functions whose derivative is continuous). By construction $I^{1/2}f_\delta=g_\delta$, so $|I^{1/2}f_\delta|=|g_\delta|\le 1/\sqrt\pi$, i.e. $f_\delta\in\mathcal F$.

Using (1),
$$\int_0^1 f_\delta=\frac{1}{\sqrt\pi}\int_0^1\frac{g_\delta(t)}{\sqrt{1-t}}\,dt=\frac{1}{\pi}\int_0^1\frac{dt}{\sqrt{1-t}}-\frac{1}{\sqrt\pi}\int_0^\delta\!\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt\pi}-g_\delta(t)\right)\frac{dt}{\sqrt{1-t}}.$$
The first term equals $2/\pi$. The correction is bounded in absolute value by
$$\frac{1}{\sqrt\pi}\cdot\frac{1}{\sqrt\pi}\int_0^\delta\frac{dt}{\sqrt{1-t}}\le\frac{1}{\pi}\cdot\frac{\delta}{\sqrt{1-\delta}}\xrightarrow[\delta\to0]{}0.$$
Hence $\int_0^1 f_\delta\to 2/\pi$ as $\delta\to0$.

Combining the upper bound (2) with the approximating sequence, 
$$\sup_{f\in\mathcal F}\left|\int_0^1 f(x)\,dx\right|=\frac{2}{\pi}.$$

